# [Remote Cache Server](https://youtu.be/_UnRVsvGSjg)
This is something big projects built with Gradle can leverage, you can cache the configuration phase outputs, saving you build time. Gradle consists of three phases, Initialization in which
it reads gradle.settings to identify included builds and projects, so it determines the project structure. Configuration, applies plugins and creates the directed acyclic graph which is a 
some sort of tree that determines the order and dependendencies between tasks, and finally the execution phase, it executes the task defined in the previous phase. You can cache youe 
configuration outputs remotely, if you have Gradle enterprise they offer you their Build Cache Server, if you don't have enterprise access you can manage your own cache server with persitent
storage, using Jar, Docker and Kubernetes, this is called "Gradle Build Cache Node" is a dedicated server application that operates as a remote backend for Gradle’s task output caching. It 
allows teams and continuous integration (CI) systems to share compiled classes, test results, and other build outputs to drastically reduce build times across different machines. You can 
also use the Gradle plugin that Androidx wrote that is backed by either Google Cloud Storage or AWS S3 buckets, [this is an implementation](https://github.com/androidx/gcp-gradle-build-cache)
of the Gradle Remote Cache, AndroidX(jetpack libraries) uses this plugin.
